  :adr/id "adr-2609151600-the-command-surface-is-kotoba"
  :adr/title "The command surface is two Kotoba components, built by amu without a JVM; the Clojure stays as the oracle until the consumers cut over"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-09-15"
  :adr/related "adr-2609151200-the-contract-is-one-repository"
  :adr/body "## What was asked

JVM 依存なし、kotoba、amu native compiler のみ。

## What was measured first (amu 73ddd26, 2026-09-15)

Whole-component, not a decision core (kotoba-lang q9-migration.edn v3,
`:decision-only-slices-allowed false`). The walls, each reproduced with a
one-file probe before any code was written:

- `[:list T]` cannot grow (`conj` refused by name), `map`/`filter` take
  vector-i64 only, and the native-AOT target refuses every typed collection:
  `[:list :string]`, `[:map :i64 :i64]`, `[:map :i64 :string]` all answer
  \"typed values currently require the kotoba-script web target, typed Wasm
  target, or qualified native string/scalar-record/option-i64/result-i64
  features\". A scalar record compiles to aarch64 and runs.
- `typed-map-keys` does not lower on wasm32; `string-index-of` is not
  qualified there; `throw`/`try` lower on the KIR interpreter only.
- The browser host admits 256 typed literals per module (300 string
  literals in one module: `too many typed ABI literals`), a module's string
  literals total 64 KiB, `case` desugars to nested `if`s and 211 clauses
  exhaust the host stack, a function takes five parameters.
- `string-length` is the UTF-8 byte count on every backend and
  `string-code-point-at` refuses an offset inside a code point.
- The compiled fuel budget (default 512) is per instance; a registry scan is
  ~2,700 and `amu test` runs every test on one instance, so it cannot gate
  this component.

## Decision

Two components, `cloud.itonami.commands` and `cloud.itonami.repo-profile`,
as `.kotoba`, compiled `amu compile --jvm-free --target wasm32 --fuel 5000000`
and shipped built under dist/ with amu's host beside them. Every dynamic
value is a `:document`; the tables are chunks of eight commands, each one EDN
string read at access time (27 + 5 + 1 literals); refusals are values with
the oracle's `:type` and data; host effects are `{:needs …}` answered by the
guest adapter; a command document is the projection a front end reads. The
generated `commands.kotoba` is the `.in` with both tables spliced in, so the
registry and the code that resolves it are one artifact.

JVM-free is measured, not declared: `amu check`, `amu compile`, the build
script, the fresh-instance test runner and the parity harness all run with a
trapping `java`/`clojure`/`clj` stub on PATH and the trap never fires.
deps.edn declares no org.clojure/clojure, no Maven coordinate, no JVM test
runner.

The Clojure oracles (`cloud.itonami.app.commands` / `.repo-profile`) stay,
required by cloud-itonami-app (JVM) and app/mobile (ClojureScript) until
those cut over. Parity: test/kotoba_parity_nbb.cljk, 588 checks over 26 argv
cases, every registry command's usage and name, six searches, 22 profile
texts and nine selections -- 0 failures; a perturbed oracle turns 2 of them
red. It found two real disagreements on the way: the cljs oracle encoded `*`
as %2A where URLEncoder does not (fixed in the oracle), and blank search terms.

## Native AOT: blocked, by the compiler's own sentence

The `aarch64-macos` target is `:blocked` for both components at the refusal
quoted above. The gap to name in the language surface plan is typed
collections (list / map) on native-aot. The components are not reduced to
scalar predicates to make that target green.

## What the consumer cutover looks like

cloud-itonami-cli: require `cloud.itonami.commands.guest` and
`cloud.itonami.repo-profile.guest` instead of the `.app.` namespaces, await
`load!` before dispatch. Same names, same values, same throws."}]
